Actually, let me reconsider the RMSNorm kernel. I'm using 256 threads with __launch_bounds__(256). This limits the maximum number of threads per block to 256. But the original used 1024 threads with __launch_bounds__(1024).

With __launch_bounds__(256), the compiler can allocate more registers per thread (up to 255 instead of 64 for 1024 threads). This can help with ILP and reduce register spilling.

But there's a subtlety: __launch_bounds__ also tells the compiler the minimum number of blocks per SM. With __launch_bounds__(256), the default is 1 block per SM. If I specify __launch_bounds__(256, 4), it would tell the compiler that at least 4 blocks will be active per SM, allowing it to use fewer registers per thread.

For the RMSNorm kernel, we have 1 block per row and typically 1 row per kernel call. So only 1 block is active at a time. With __launch_bounds__(256), the compiler assumes at least 1 block per SM, which is correct.

Actually, __launch_bounds__(maxThreadsPerBlock, minBlocksPerMultiprocessor) where minBlocksPerMultiprocessor defaults to 1 if not specified. This is fine for our use case.

